1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Charles Busk found that in 1889.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
In August 1977, Martin Gardner described R S A in Scientific American, with a challenge: a message locked with a 129 digit number.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Rivest estimated that, with the best methods of the day, a 125 digit number would take 40 quadrillion years.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
But better methods came, and in 1994 about 600 volunteers, with some 1,600 computers, cracked it.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The message read: The magic words are squeamish ossifrage.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
So keys grew. Public factoring records are under 300 digits. A 2,048 bit key has 617.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And no one has proved that factoring is hard. There's just no fast way known on ordinary computers.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Run the same maths the other way, and you get something just as useful: a signature.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Diffie and Hellman proposed digital signatures in 1976, and R S A made them work.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Bob locks a message with his private 413. Anyone can open it with his public 17.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Signing 1977 gives 788. And 788 to the power of 17 gives back 1977.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Only the holder of 413 could have made 788, so it proves who signed.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
In practice you sign a short fingerprint of the message, called a hash.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Signatures also close a gap in key exchange: an attacker in the middle.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Mallory sits between Alice and Bob and runs one exchange with each, posing as the other.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Each thinks they share a key with the other. Both share one with Mallory, who reads everything.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Plain Diffie–Hellman can't tell who is really on the other end.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
On the web, the proof is a certificate: the site's public key, signed by an authority your browser trusts.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
There's a twist, kept secret for more than twenty years.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
In January 1970, at Britain's G C H Q, James Ellis wrote a secret report on non-secret encryption.
